CONTEXT. The previous page sets up a lunar occultation of a star by orthographic projection. The fundamental plane passes through the Earth's centre and is perpendicular to the star direction. Coordinates are in arc-seconds of parallax: x points east along the parallel to the equator, y points north.

In this plane the Moon's centre moves in a straight line:
- inclination ζ to the x-axis;
- speed n per second of sidereal time;
- at time Θ it crosses x=0 at ordinate η.

The observer (radius vector P, geocentric latitude φ, star hour angle h, declination δ) projects to:
- a = P cosφ sin h = P sin z sin M
- b = P sinφ cosδ − P cosφ sinδ cos h = P sin z cos M

Polar form relative to the Moon's centre at Θ: a = q cosμ, b − η = q sinμ. The Moon's radius is ϱ.

At contact (time Θ+θ):
- n θ cosζ − q cosμ = −ϱ sin(ζ+ν)
- n θ sinζ − q sinμ = ϱ cos(ζ+ν)

Hence:
- (III) cosν = q sin(ζ−μ)/ϱ
- (IV) θ = q cos(ζ−μ+ν)/(n cosν) = (q/n)cos(ζ−μ) − (ϱ/n) sinν
- sinν > 0 gives immersion (Eintritt), sinν < 0 gives emersion (Austritt).

THIS PAGE, PART 1 (geometric meaning). Three points in the projection:
- point 1: Moon's centre at Θ;
- point 2: Moon's centre at Θ+θ;
- point 3: the observer, which is also the limb point where contact occurs.

Segments:
- 1→2 has length nθ and direction ζ from the abscissa line;
- 1→3 has length q and direction μ;
- 3→2 has length ϱ and direction ζ+90°+ν.

The vector identity (1→2) = (1→3) + (3→2), resolved on the axes, is exactly the contact system above (my check). The direction from the projected Earth centre to point 3 is 90°−M; this is the projected upward vertical, since (a, b) = P sin z (sin M, cos M).

Directions of great circles through the star, counted from east towards north:
- 0 = parallel to the equator, eastward;
- 90° = towards the north celestial pole;
- p = parallel to the ecliptic, eastward;
- 90°−M = vertical upward;
- ζ+90°+ν = towards the Moon's centre.

Because star and Moon are only ~ϱ apart, Gauss transfers the same directions to the Moon's apparent centre ('ohne Bedenken'). From the Moon's centre the star then lies in direction ζ−90°+ν.

Measured from the southern limb point (direction 270°), the star's position angle is:
- ζ+ν eastward with respect to the equator;
- ζ+ν−p with respect to the ecliptic;
- ζ+ν+M with respect to the lowest point (direction 270°−M).

All three follow by subtraction mod 360° (checked).

PART 2 (numerical example: immersion and emersion at Göttingen). Constants:
- log P = 3.55854 + 9.99911 (Earth-radius factor) = 3.55765, so P ≈ 3612";
- log cosφ = 9.79562 and log sinφ = 9.89261, giving φ' ≈ 51°20' (geocentric latitude of Göttingen);
- log P cosφ = 3.35327 and log P sinφ = 3.45026;
- log sinδ = 9.44356 and log cosδ = 9.98258, so δ ≈ +16°7' (my guess: Aldebaran);
- ζ = 10°11'22", log C.ϱ = 7.00274 (ϱ ≈ 993.7"), log C.n = 0.21372 (n ≈ 0.611"/s);
- η = +1810.2, Θ = 22h27m6.9s (from the previous page).

Procedure: assume an hour angle h and compute a, b, q, μ, ν, θ. The local time equals h/15; Gauss's times are evidently star-hour-angle sidereal times. Subtracting Θ+θ from it gives the implied longitude. Iterate until this equals the true value (30m23s, reached on the next page).

Trial 1, immersion, h = 327°45':
- a = −1203.5 (log 3.08050n);
- P sinφ cosδ = 2709.2 and the second term is −529.7, so b = 2179.5 and b−η = 369.3 (log 2.56738);
- μ = 162°56'35", log cos = 9.98046, so log q = 3.10004;
- ζ−μ = 207°14'47", log sin = 9.66070n;
- log cosν = 9.76348n, so ν = 125°27'20";
- ζ−μ+ν = 332°42'7", log cos = 9.94872;
- θ = −3155s = −52m35.0s;
- Θ+θ = 21h34m31.9s, local time 21h51m0s, implied longitude 16m28.1s.

Trial 2, immersion, h = 329°53':
- a = −1131.6 (log 3.05377n);
- second term −541.8, so b = 2167.4 and b−η = 357.2 (log 2.55291);
- μ = 162°29'3", log cos = 9.97938, so log q = 3.07439;
- ζ−μ = 207°42'19", log sin = 9.66738n;
- log cosν = 9.74451n, so ν ≈ 123°44';
- log(q/cosν) = 3.32988n, and log cos(ζ−μ+ν) ≈ 9.94363;
- θ ≈ −3070.6s = −51m10.6s;
- Θ+θ = 21h35m56.3s, local time 21h59m32s, implied longitude 23m35.7s.

Trial 2 also gives the altitude and position angle:
- tan M = a/b, so M = −27°34'23";
- log P sin z = log b − log cosM = 3.38830;
- log sin z = 3.38830 − 3.55854 (unreduced P) = 9.82976, so altitude 47°29';
- position angles: 156°28' from the equator, 147°13' from the ecliptic, 128°53' from the vertical.

Emersion, h = 353°23':
- a = −259.9 (log 2.41482n);
- second term −622.2, so b = 2087.0 and b−η = 276.8 (log 2.44217);
- μ = 133°11'50" (here q is taken from b−η and sinμ: log sin = 9.86273, log q = 2.57944);
- ζ−μ = 236°59'32", log sin = 9.92355n;
- log cosν = 9.50573n; sinν < 0 is chosen, so ν = −108°41'20";
- ζ−μ+ν = 128°18'12", log cos = 9.79227n;
- log θ = 3.07970, so θ = +1201.4s = +20m1.4s;
- Θ+θ = 22h47m8.3s, local time 23h33m32s, implied longitude 46m23.7s.

The next page completes the regula-falsi iteration: h = 331°56'20" and 347°49'30" both give longitude 30m23.0s.
